Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City http: Ilwww.dilg.com.ph 07 OILG OPINION NO. 05 HON. JEREMY JONAHMAR G. PELAEZ Vice Governor Province of Misamis Oriental Dear Vice Gov. Pelaez: This has reference to your letter dated 12 September 2019 requesting for legal opinion relative to Sangguniang Panlalawigan Resolution No. 34-2019. Your inquiry is relative to the refusal of the Provincial Board Secretary, Atty. Ernesto R. Sotto, Jr., to certify the correctness of the SP Resolution 34-2019. SP Resolution 34-2019 intends to remove the name of Atty. Sotto, Jr. from all payroll transactions for salaries and other benefits of the elected members of the 18th SP and to transfer his name in the payroll for salaries and other benefits of permanent employees of the SP effective immediately. The reasons in the said Resolution are: (I) to avoid any circumstances that might cause the delay in the processing of the payroll for salaries and other benefits of the elected members of the SP; and (2) to ensure that the processing for the salaries and other benefits of permanent employees be given priority and be processed promptly on a given scheduled period of time. It is also mentioned that Atty. Sotto, Jr. admitted during a regular session that he failed to submit his daily time records (DTR) for three (3) consecutive years and that he still claimed monthly salaries during those years. In response to the foregoing, Atty. Sotto, Jr. sent a Memorandum addressed to the Honorable Members of the said SP mainly alleging the following: S. 2020 • • The subject SP Resolution is an encroachment on the administrative authority of the Vice Governor and as such beyond the authority of the SP to do and thus, it is violative of Republic Act No. 7160 (RA 7160), otherwise known as the Local Government Code of 1991. The subject resolution shall be addressed to the sound discretion of the Vice Governor as the Administrative head of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan; The subject SP Resolution is unfair, oppressive, and unreasonable. It was a product of a proceeding where the Provincial Board Secretary was subjected to questioning that was only answerable by yes or no, without giving him the opportunity to explain;

• • The subject SP Resolution contains wrong facts particularly on the failure to submit DTR despite manifestation of an SP member of Memorandum No. JJGP 2019-011 dated 01 July 2019, which authorizes flexible time arrangement and exemption from biometric for the undersigned; The subject SP Resolution was already signed by the author when it reached his office preventing him from editing errors and correcting errors. Thus, this process encroaches upon his authority to exercise due diligence in the performance of his work and in verifying the contents of Resolutions and Ordinances before certifying to its correctness; and Several whereas clauses in the said resolution are not found in the Minutes of the pertinent session and recordings of said proceedings. Our view has been consistent that the Vice Governor has the administrative authority over the Sanggunian officials and employees. Thus, it is within the authority of the Vice Governor to act upon whether or not the payroll transactions are to be removed from that of the SP members to that of the SP permanent employees. In DILG Legal Opinion No. 032-94, dated 02 March 1994, it is stated: "Relevantly, under Section 445(a) of RA 7160, the municipal vice-mayor shall 'appoint all officials and employees of the sangguniang bayan, except those whose manner of appointment is specifically provided in this Code. ' Considering that sanggunian employees are appointees of the vice-mayor, they are, therefore, within the latter's administrative supervisory authority, as all their duties and functions relate to services to be rendered in the sanggunian. It being so, the imposition of disciplinary actions/measures against officials and employees of the sanggunian are within the jurisdiction of the vice-mayor. " Notwithstanding, we are also of the view that the Sanggunian is not precluded from passing a resolution on the subject matter considering the provisions in RA 7160, to wit: SECTION 468. Powers, Duties, Functions and Compensation . — (a) The sangguniang panlalawigan, as the legislative body of the province, shall enact ordinances, approve resolutions and appropriate funds for the general welfare of the province and its inhabitants pursuant to Section 16 of this Code in the proper exercise of the corporate powers of the province as provided for under Section 22 of this Code, and shall: (1) Approve ordinances and pass resolutions necessary for an efficient and effective provincial government and, in this connection, shall: xxx

(vii) Subject to the provisions of this Code and pertinent laws, determine the powers and duties of officials and employees of the province; (viii) Determine the positions and the salaries, wages, allowances and other emoluments and benefits of officials and employees paid wholly or mainly from provincial funds and provide for expenditures necessary for the proper conduct of programs, projects, services, and activities of the provincial government; xxx It has been settled what a resolution is, as opposed to an ordinance. In the case of Municipality of Paraäaque vs. V.M. Realty Corporationl, the Supreme Court states: A municipal ordinance is different from a resolution. An ordinance is a law, but a resolution is merely a declaration of the sentiment or opinion of a lawmaking body on a specific matter. An ordinance possesses a general and permanent character, but a resolution is temporary in nature. Additionally, the two are enacted differently — a third reading is necessary for an ordinance, but not for a resolution, unless decided otherwise by a majority of all the Sanggunian members. Being a mere sentiment or opinion of a lawmaking body, we are of the view that said SP resolution is still subject to the discretion of the Vice Governor, who is the administrative authority on the matter. As to the refusal of the Sangguniang Secretary to certify the correctness of the draft SP resolution, we are of the view that said refusal shall not bar the SP from passing said resolution. Otherwise, it puts the SP at the mercy of or into submission to the Sanggunian Secretary, which is devoid of legal basis. We hope to have enlightened you on the matter. Thank you. 1 G.R. NO. 127820 July 20, 1998 Very truly yours, By the authority of the Secretary MARIVEL SACENDONCILLO, CESO 11 Underse retary for Local Government
